#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120934} :activity/id #uuid "18514bce-156b-56a7-b919-0aa8cd6b4e23" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048770 :topic/id #uuid "bd442719-9650-5137-aa05-949d5ce4809c" :topic/math-academy-id 1819} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121142 :tutorial/content "Finding the **cube root** of a number is the reverse of cubing a number. We express the cube root using the **radical symbol** $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}.$\n\nFor example, the \"cube root of $8$\" equals $\\color{blue}2$ since \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^3 = {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, we write $\\sqrt[3]{8}={\\color{blue}2}.$\n\nSome basic cube roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{0} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{0}^{3}} & = & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{1}^{3}} & = & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{2}^{3}} & = & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{27} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{3}^{3}} & = & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{64} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{4}^{3}} & = & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{125} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}} & = & 5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{10}^{3}} & = & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA **perfect cube** is a number whose cube root is a whole number. So, the numbers \n\n$$\n0,\\qquad 1,\\qquad  8,\\qquad  27,\\qquad  64,\\qquad 125, \\qquad 1\\,000\n$$\n\n are all perfect cubes. You need to remember them." :tutorial/id #uuid "82bf149b-fb3b-506e-9c7b-3d6716081eaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8377 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bc943f83-ca68-59a5-8d04-1f3be7f4da3e" :step/math-academy-id 554687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119958 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119524 :question/id #uuid "71372a52-1de2-5535-9216-6d90db0afc49" :question/math-academy-id "e-13374"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32268c05-9166-5798-81fc-ac3a99d966e0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475979}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Cubed Integer"} :step/id #uuid "41a11614-97fd-50d6-ae34-9ed01ba0df21" :step/math-academy-id 554688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119959 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119525 :question/id #uuid "60f95f30-9f10-5fe5-b5b6-f7b74e219a57" :question/math-academy-id "e-13375"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e588edf8-345e-57e7-b3ee-9c3666da08e6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493945}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Positive Perfect Cube"} :step/id #uuid "1893b834-1078-578f-b788-398a16705469" :step/math-academy-id 554692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120937}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121143 :tutorial/content "Unlike the square root, it *is* possible to find the cube root of a negative number.\n\nFor example, $\\sqrt[3]{-8}={\\color{blue}{-2}}$ because\n\n$$\n-8 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})^3 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}}).\n$$\n\nSome basic cube roots of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-1} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{3}} & = & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-8} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-2)^{3}} & = & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-27} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}} & = & -3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-64} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-4)^{3}} & = & -4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-125} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-5)^{3}} & = & -5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-1\\,000} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-10)^{3}} & = & -10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the numbers \n\n$$\n-1,\\qquad  -8,\\qquad  -27,\\qquad  -64,\\qquad -125, \\qquad -1\\,000\n$$\n\nare all perfect cubes." :tutorial/id #uuid "02c87b9c-9741-553f-a98b-da7832928f6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8378 :tutorial/title "The Cube Root of a Negative Perfect Cube"} :step/id #uuid "8b332403-62f5-5fe0-ac76-52a66e2c10b5" :step/math-academy-id 554696 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119960 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119526 :question/id #uuid "5c5c9efb-7bff-5177-a736-b21ce6410871" :question/math-academy-id "e-13376"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b9e5026-ab54-5f35-a8da-b647c0f68c1e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475964}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Negative Perfect Cube"} :step/id #uuid "b172821f-393c-54f4-bec3-98560d9d0245" :step/math-academy-id 554697 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121144 :tutorial/content "Let's calculate $(\\sqrt[3]{64})^3$ by performing each operation one step at a time:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\color{blue}64}\\right)^3 = \\left(\\sqrt[3]{{\\color{red}4}^3}\\right)^3 = {\\color{red}4}^3 = {\\color{blue}64}\n$$\n\nNotice that cubing the cube root gives the number under the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\sqrt[3]{64} \\right)^3 = 64\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. If $\\color{blue}\\square$ is any number, then\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\color{blue}\\square}\\right)^3 = {\\color{blue}\\square}.\n$$\n\nThe cube and the cube root cancel each other out, and we're left with the number that we started with.\n\n![](images/78/784f43f7253e1ed6fa394462a172e22571af06a5318b424ba2eab2157333c6fe.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "900df988-f097-53fe-8a55-39641f798d4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8379 :tutorial/title "Cubing a Cube Root"} :step/id #uuid "b06bda82-f4c5-5f02-bffa-cfb125aada53" :step/math-academy-id 554701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119961 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119527 :question/id #uuid "fc571b6d-1dfe-5344-99c0-d6d2f5d1f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c331c742-c620-5e3c-ae77-37de9ded75cc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493942}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube of the Cube Root of a Positive or Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "2768c295-f0d4-5823-a374-5050e8220981" :step/math-academy-id 554702 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121145 :tutorial/content "We can think of the cube root geometrically. For instance, let's represent the number $8$ by a collection of eight items:\n\n![](images/36/3677ea5b5f875e12fdcc6376f6b29b43a663495d20a3fbf69b4937cc61661d79.png)\n\nNow, we can pile them up in the form of a cube where each side is equal to $\\sqrt[3]{8}={\\color{blue}2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "859f5c79-ff9d-5b6d-91b3-305863a271c1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8380 :tutorial/title "A Geometric Approach to the Cube Root"} :step/id #uuid "f9786bea-a98c-554e-ac43-50ebe1956098" :step/math-academy-id 554705}] :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120026}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493995} :activity/id #uuid "9b8dddd1-da8a-515b-b440-7da08015b94d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048770 :topic/id #uuid "bd442719-9650-5137-aa05-949d5ce4809c" :topic/math-academy-id 1819} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11db075b-d65e-51e5-8dca-f8e9ab1adb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493421} {:answer/id #uuid "5c730b9a-1755-532b-b8d1-24d3aece98b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493533} {:answer/id #uuid "671ff086-c9db-58f6-af7a-4edb329a1ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493544} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0b316d-8f3a-5a45-a97f-136faefce7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493609} {:answer/id #uuid "f015c16e-7bc0-5eb8-9649-05d8fe751886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493609} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a4987cd-b000-5d3d-b94d-9c8d5cd48460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e1e6dba-6746-5b7e-9ed7-77e1ee7e8c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-71494" :question/problem "$(\\sqrt[3]{1,000})^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "The cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[3]{1,000})^{3}=1,000.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3b1fdc86-b563-5301-a67d-cba49bff52be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cbd15cf-50e0-5f6c-a023-66b7777a5ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493467} :answer-field/id #uuid "77156935-a293-5f77-b17e-01ad81fabeda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "03b02208-a47d-5484-9a1d-0e9d1a5e06da" :question/math-academy-id "q-186156" :question/problem "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $125$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{125}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "734e7353-a71f-5a59-9552-8bb9d82e349e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa5916f5-b95b-5530-9d9f-19d0168e2209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493658} :answer-field/id #uuid "f90a68c7-dc57-5362-b958-0dc11f3f0c40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aafcb24f-fde2-52b2-b44a-c7d7fddbc683" :question/math-academy-id "q-186155" :question/problem "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $27$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{27}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d4a52c70-3918-5a00-b200-12e071807393" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494013}}] :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493383}]}
